<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takasugi Shinsaku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takasugi Shinsaku was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Chōshū domain and a leader of the movement to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>. A student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>, he founded the Kiheitai in 1863, a militia that admitted commoners as well as samurai. In 1865 he took control of Chōshū for the radicals, and in 1866 he helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s second expedition, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanmon-straits">Kanmon Straits</a> to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a>. He died of tuberculosis in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takasugi-shinsaku.jpg" alt="Takasugi Shinsaku" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinsaku_Takasugi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Takasugi was born on 27 September 1839 in Hagi, the castle town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a>, the first son of Takasugi Kochūta, a middle-ranked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. He became a favourite student at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>'s private school, the Shōka Sonjuku, and in 1858 entered the Shōheikō at Edo. He visited his teacher in jail after his arrest in the Ansei Purge, and Shōin was executed on 21 November 1859. In January 1860 he married Inoue Masa in a match arranged by his parents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Takasugi was among the most extreme advocates in Chōshū of expelling foreigners. In 1862 the domain sent him secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, where the effects of European imperialism during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> convinced him that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> must strengthen itself or share the fate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>. He was implicated in the attack on the British legation in Edo on 31 January 1863. His Kiheitai of June 1863 had 300 soldiers, about half of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, but he was dismissed as its leader about two months later after a deadly clash with another unit.</p><p>After the Western powers attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a> in September 1864, the domain made him Director of Military Affairs and, at 25, entrusted him with negotiating peace. He concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> must learn from the West, rearmed the Kiheitai with modern rifles and turned the movement to expel the barbarians and revere the Emperor against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> itself. When conservatives took power after the shogunate's expedition of 1864, he gathered about a dozen followers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo">Yamagata Aritomo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> and Inoue Kaoru, and won the civil war that began on 13 January 1865. On 7 June 1866 Chōshū defeated the Second Chōshū expedition on four fronts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His tuberculosis worsened in October 1866. Nursed by his mistress O-Uno, later the nun Tani Baisho, and the poet Nomura Bōtō, he died on 17 May 1867, aged 28, and was buried near the Kiheitai's camp at Mount Kiyomizu in Yoshida. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> followed a year later. In 1884 his comrades raised funds for the hermitage Tōgyō-an beside his grave, which became a National Historic Monument in 1934. Many of his friends and followers joined the genrō, the senior statesmen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a>, and Hagi remembers him for his reforms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Takasugi Shinsaku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1865 <b>Takasugi Shinsaku</b> and the Kiheitai militia ousted Chōshū’s conservatives, and on 7 March 1866 Sakamoto Ryōma brought Kido Takayoshi and Saigō Takamori together in the Satsuma–Chōshū Alliance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeated in Kyoto in 1864, Choshu was rebuilt by radicals around <b>Takasugi Shinsaku's</b> mixed militia, beat the shogunate's army in 1866 and allied with Satsuma.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>In the second shogunate expedition against Choshu in 1866, Choshu forces under <b>Takasugi Shinsaku</b> crossed the straits; Kokura burned its castle and withdrew inland to Kawara.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokura-domain">Kokura Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kikuya Yokochō. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.96,130.94&amp;year=1857" aria-label="Shimonoseki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Takasugi Shinsaku"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5771 4625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5771 4625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.96,130.94&amp;year=1857">What was here in 1857? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
